#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})j \\\\\n&= 2xi + 6y^{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= 2(\\sin t)i + 6(e^{t})^{2}j \\\\\n&= 2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t})j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + e^{t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j) \\cdot (\\cos ti + e^{t}j) \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t\\cos t + 6e^{2t} \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\sin 2t + 6e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t \\, \\mathbf j +t^3 \\, \\mathbf k$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(xz\\ln y)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(xz\\ln y)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(xz\\ln y)k \\\\\n&= z\\ln yi + \\frac{xz}{y}j + x\\ln yk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2$, $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j + 3t^{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk) \\cdot (2ti + j + 3t^{2}k) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln t) \\cdot 2t + (t^{4}) \\cdot 1 + (t^{2}\\ln t) \\cdot 3t^{2} \\\\\n&= 2t^{4}\\ln t + t^{4} + 3t^{4}\\ln t \\\\\n&= t^{4}(5\\ln t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124193 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. Here $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\langle2x,-2y,-1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'=\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle$. Thus $$\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle2\\sin t,-2\\cos t,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle=4\\sin t\\cos t-1.$$ Evaluating at $t=\\pi/4$ gives $4(\\sqrt2/2)^2-1=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124322 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. At $t=\\pi$, $\\mathbf r(\\pi)=(0,-1)$, so $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(\\pi))=\\langle-3,-1\\rangle$. Also $\\mathbf r'(\\pi)=\\langle\\cos\\pi,-\\sin\\pi\\rangle=\\langle-1,0\\rangle$. Therefore the required rate is $(-3)(-1)+(-1)(0)=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81771d9a-1173-5d93-8736-a5493809a044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2t} + 2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122249} {:answer/id #uuid "c51dfbf4-8cec-51f6-b4af-d51165c85767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t} + e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122250} {:answer/id #uuid "d3015426-1046-57c7-a140-519215e56f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122251} {:answer/id #uuid "d4138f64-e477-50c9-91e2-a203e3236416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t} - 2e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122252} {:answer/id #uuid "40615ba7-fb40-55a4-8395-0174697ae8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2t} - 2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40615ba7-fb40-55a4-8395-0174697ae8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2t} - 2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122253} :answer-field/id #uuid "242c75f3-bc80-513a-8ebc-f916daea8e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ea55a51-bc91-526a-9277-7689869bc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-36211" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + {e}^{- t} \\, \\mathbf{j} ,$, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} t} [ f ( \\mathbf{r} ( t ) ) ] .$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=\\langle2x,2y\\rangle$, so along the curve it is $\\langle2e^t,2e^{-t}\\rangle$. Since $\\mathbf r'=\\langle e^t,-e^{-t}\\rangle$, the vector chain rule gives $$\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)=2e^{2t}-2e^{-2t}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4571305-e385-58b0-8ec5-ee294ea0c6b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123995} {:answer/id #uuid "ec50a717-1457-57bb-a42e-095372bb3cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123996} {:answer/id #uuid "01cfe9d5-ebfc-54b9-870d-10c08f16c6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123997} {:answer/id #uuid "05ebf4f1-974c-5990-bf8e-56f518f6aeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - \\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123998} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9f76d0-c631-5487-bb51-f9a7d0ab3978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec50a717-1457-57bb-a42e-095372bb3cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123996} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac100fd-6275-58e5-9f39-0e3451031c82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" :question/math-academy-id "q-94872" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} t} [ f ( \\mathbf{r} ( t ) ) ]$ at $t = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) = \\sin   y \\, \\mathbf{i} + x \\cos   y \\, \\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r} ( t ) = \\frac{t}{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\, \\mathbf{j} .$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At $t=\\pi/2$, the point on the curve is $(\\pi/4,\\pi/2)$. Thus $\\nabla f=\\langle1,0\\rangle$, while $\\mathbf r'=\\langle1/2,1\\rangle$. Their dot product gives the required derivative: $1\\cdot\\tfrac12+0\\cdot1=\\tfrac12$."}]}
